{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 51, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass on the very day, HaShem brought out, etc.</b> This verse has already been said above (verse 41), and there are several differences between these two verses. For above it says ״יָצְאוּ כָּל צִבְאוֹת ה׳״ (Exodus 12:41), which implies as though they went out on their own, whereas here it says ״הוֹצִיא ה׳״ — HaShem brought them out. Above it says that all the hosts of HaShem went out, and did not mention the children of Israel, whereas here it says ״הוֹצִיא ה׳ אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — HaShem brought out the children of Israel. And furthermore, it says ״עַל צִבְאֹתָם״ (Exodus 12:51), which implies that ״צִבְאוֹת״ is not the same as the children of Israel." "And what seems closest to me to say about this is that the mixed multitude who converted and went out from Egypt on their own — them the verse calls ״צִבְאוֹת ה׳״, for converts are beloved before the Holy One, blessed be He. Even though they are not included among the children of Israel, they are nevertheless ״צִבְאוֹת ה׳״ just like Israel. Therefore in the first verse it says ״יָצְאוּ כָּל צִבְאוֹת ה׳״ concerning the converts, who went out on their own and HaShem did not bring them out. But in the second verse it speaks of the children of Israel themselves, whom HaShem brought out ״עַל צִבְאֹתָם״ — in addition to those hosts who had already gone out on their own. And therefore in parashat Va'era (Exodus 7:4) it says ״וְהוֹצֵאתִי אֶת צִבְאֹתַי אֶת עַמִּי בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, so that you should not understand ״וְהוֹצֵאתִי אֶת צִבְאֹתַי״ as referring to the congregation of converts — therefore it was necessary to specify ״אֶת עַמִּי״. For without a doubt Israel too are called ״צְבָאוֹת״, and our Sages of blessed memory said that it is a compound word: ״צָבָא אוֹת״ — that is to say, a host for whom I have a sign. And they said this concerning the sign of Sabbath, circumcision, and tefillin, for all three are called ״אוֹת״, and the reason is well known. And regarding both of them it says ״וַיְהִי בְּעֶצֶם הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה״, for concerning the mixed multitude it was necessary to say this — that they went out in the sight of all and did not fear Pharaoh their king; and concerning Israel it was necessary to say this to indicate that the exodus from Egypt was literally by day, as it is written (Numbers 33:3) ״מִמָּחֳרַת הַפֶּסַח יָצְאוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״. And even though it says (Deuteronomy 16:1) ״הוֹצִיאֲךָ ה׳ מִמִּצְרַיִם לָיְלָה״, Rashi and the Ramban explained that at night He gave them permission to leave, and Mahariva explained that the word ״לַיְלָה״ refers back to ״וְעָשִׂיתָ פֶּסַח״."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:48.513691804Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}